WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.380
Cognitive Load Theory is a practical framework for designing learning around the limits of working memory.

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.010
It starts with a simple reality: when people learn something new,

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.630
they can only hold and process a limited amount of information at one time.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:17.920
If a lesson asks learners to manage too many new elements, unclear examples,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.560
busy visuals, and unfamiliar terms all at once,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.190
learning can fail even when the learners are capable and motivated.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.510
At a practical level,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.470
working memory is the mental workspace learners use while they are trying

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.120
to understand something right now.

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.080
Long-term memory is where organized knowledge, patterns, procedures, and concepts are stored.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.040
Learning happens when instruction helps people process new information well enough to

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.020
build useful structures in long-term memory.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:44.980
Once those structures exist, future tasks become easier to understand and perform.

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.620
Cognitive load is not a judgment about intelligence.

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.600
It is an instructional design concern.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.570
A confusing course can overwhelm learners with presentation friction,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.880
even when the topic itself is reasonable.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.830
A difficult topic can also be taught in a way that manages the load carefully,

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:02.140
building foundations before asking for independent performance.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.090
The difference matters because learners often blame themselves when the design is the real problem.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.730
The goal is not to make learning effortless.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:13.360
Some mental effort is necessary because understanding and skill require work.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.670
The goal is to make effort productive.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.970
Good design reduces unnecessary burden, sequences complexity, uses examples well,

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:21.940
and helps learners focus on the relationships that matter.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.900
Cognitive Load Theory gives designers a language for deciding which difficulty belongs

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.860
to the task and which difficulty was accidentally created by the lesson.
